module v2.test.claim.coercion.checker_evaluator_cast_fork

// CLASS-ROW WITNESS: does v2 carry the checker/evaluator cast fork that #16224 (9443e64f) closed in v1?
// (class row gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.the_checker_admits_a_cast_the_evaluator_refuses, whose untested
// follow-up names this sibling: the v2 checker and the v2 evaluator are separate procedures -- the checker's
// cast admission is v2.compiler.infer's `infer_transform_cast_optional`, which consults
// v2.std.coercion.coercion_cast_crossing, and the cast's realization is
// v2.extdeps.runtimes.v2_evaluator's `v2_eval_coerce` -- and nothing in the corpus PAIRS their verdicts on
// one authored cast.)
//
// THE FORK, stated as a read: for one authored program, the checker ADMITS the cast and the evaluator's own
// step REFUSES it. That pair is what #16224 closed in v1, where v1's checker abstained on a cast of an
// Int-kernel value into a different range-refined Int carrier and v1's `eval_cast` refused it at run time
// with `cannot cast <value> to <carrier>`. The v1 witness is
// dag/test/claim/machine_intake/refined_int_cast_fork_witness.dag.
//
// WHY THIS IS NOT ALREADY ANSWERED BY THE v2 ROWS THAT EXIST.
// src/v2/test/claim/body_cast_node.dag reads the CHECKER alone -- its refinement probes stop at infer's
// verdict, and its evaluation rows hand-build the cast node and give the cast node and its operand the SAME
// hand-written facts, so the case where the two authorities could disagree is exactly the case it writes
// out. src/v2/test/claim/coercion/two_algebra_fork.dag reads the CHECKER against a hand-written expectation
// of what evaluation admits, and states in its own header that the checker is accepted as STRICTER than the
// evaluator; an expectation of strictness cannot see a refusal on the admitted side.
// src/v2/test/claim/execution/emit_host_identity_cast_native.dag runs an INT-kernel identity cast, whose
// target is the carrier itself -- the one shape no authority can disagree about. No row puts a refinement-
// carrier cast in front of BOTH procedures and reads their verdicts together.
//
// THE ONE INTERFACE (gunbc.design_argument::witness_one_interface): a probe's SOURCE STRING goes in, the
// PAIRED verdict comes out -- the checker's verdict on the resolved tree and, when the checker admits, the
// evaluator's verdict on the SAME tree, once rooted at the cast node and once at the cast's operand. Both
// arms run production: v2.compiler.refinement_discharge's `infer_and_discharge` for the checker, and
// v2.compiler.eval's `eval` over v2.extdeps.runtimes.v2_evaluator's `v2_evaluator_interpretation()` for the
// evaluator. Nothing here reimplements a verdict.
//
// NO VACUITY. A probe the checker refuses cannot form the pair, so a row asking only "is the pair absent?"
// could be satisfied by a checker that refuses EVERYTHING -- the opposite failure, the one
// src/v2/test/claim/coercion/two_algebra_fork.dag already pins as accepted design. Four kinds of row keep
// this discriminating: the two refinement controls (identity, widening) and the two route controls (kernel
// literal, kernel data reference) each name their OWN verdict and must be admitted by BOTH authorities, so a
// stricter checker and a stricter evaluator both go red; `cecf_the_control_probes_reach_the_evaluator_at_all`
// claims an operand really evaluates; and `cecf_verdict_census` prints the whole reading as a typed refusal
// reason, so a green is never taken for a reading that did not happen. An operand the evaluator refuses is
// its own reading (`OperandRefused`), not a silent NotRunnable: the checker admitted that value, and a
// consumer refusing what its producer admitted is the class -- whether the refusal lands on the cast or on
// the operand the cast wraps.
//
// WHAT THE EXECUTION SAID, so the rows below are read against a measurement and not a hope. Run by the
// remote build owner under `claim_batch --hermetic --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --entry
// src/v2/test/claim/coercion/checker_evaluator_cast_fork.dag` at 76b72a49a3a6fd1940abcb20a08d8a64403645c0
// (BuildBuddy invocation 5b6fd63e-b301-478e-95e2-7e89697cdd78): 4 pass / 5 fail, rc 1. The receipt is also on
// the class row (dag/gunbc/recurring_failure_mode/the_checker_admits_a_cast_the_evaluator_refuses.dag) and in
// the pull request body.
//   lit, xref   pre=n, chk=n, cr=find_witness_reason_no_candidate -- v2's CHECKER closes #16224's population:
//                 the crossing refuses a kernel value entering a foreign refinement before any value is
//                 realized (the reason is emitted by v2.std.find_witness at src/v2/std/find_witness.dag `find_witness`,
//                 reached from v2.std.coercion's evidence search at src/v2/std/coercion.dag `coercion_cast_exact_witness`), so the exact
//                 v1 fork -- check admits, eval_cast refuses -- cannot arise in v2 at that population.
//   ident       pre=y, chk=y, cast=y, opd=y, eop=n, eca=n, er=eval_rejected_grounding_not_derived
//               ident_deep:castfacts=derived,opdfacts=derived,opreason=eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch,
//                 refnode=at=operand_root
//   widen       pre=y, chk=y, cast=y, opd=y, eop=n, eca=n, er=eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch
//               widen_deep:castfacts=derived,opdfacts=derived,opreason=eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch,
//                 refnode=at=operand_root
//                 -- both authorities admit the crossing, and the evaluator then refuses the refined-carrier
//                 VALUE the checker admitted, at the operand the cast wraps. That is #16224's
//                 producer-admits/consumer-refuses pair one node before the cast, and it is why
//                 `cecf_no_probe_is_admitted_by_the_checker_and_refused_by_the_evaluator` is RED.
//   kern_lit    pre=y, chk=y, cast=y, opd=y, eop=y, eca=y
//   kern_ref    pre=y, chk=y, cast=y, opd=y, eop=y, eca=y
//                 -- the route controls close the artifact question: this route DOES evaluate a cast, over a
//                 literal and over a data reference alike. What the evaluator will not realize is specifically
//                 a value whose node type is a refinement over a numeric Int carrier. v2 stamps Int primitives
//                 with `integer_int_type_node()` (v2_eval_int_runtime_value at src/v2/extdeps/runtimes/
//                 v2_evaluator.dag `v2_eval_int_runtime_value`), while the acceptance gate `eval_runtime_value_acceptance_witness`
//                 (src/v2/compiler/eval.dag `eval_runtime_value_acceptance_witness`) requires `node_equal_ignoring_provenance` between the node's
//                 inferred resolved type and the value's resolved type -- a node typed as a refinement can
//                 therefore never accept the value its own operand realizes. The two refusals carry DIFFERENT
//                 reason names and each needs its own diagnosis; neither is assumed to be the other.
//   THE DEEP READING IS THE DIAGNOSIS. For BOTH controls the operand's own facts are DERIVED, the
//     refusal is located AT THE OPERAND ROOT, and the reason there is
//     eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch -- the value's stamped type against the node's declared type.
//     eval_rejected_grounding_not_derived appears only when evaluation is ROOTED AT THE CAST, which is a
//     knock-on of the same operand refusal, not a second gap: identity therefore shares widening's root
//     and is expected to go green with it, which the identity row below measures rather than assumes.
//     The earliest unjustified link is the EVALUATOR, not the checker: a checker-side arm would delete
//     the only two refined casts v2 admits (identity and widening), which is why the fix is the carrier
//     arm and not a second refusal.
//
// WHAT THE EXECUTION MEASURED AT THE FIX -- a6f79a3cb622, BuildBuddy 121d05c3-e094-4beb-9371-a4182dbc0668,
//   8 pass / 3 fail. The carrier arm did what it was built to do, and it left ONE thing over, which is
//   what the three rows and this header now describe honestly:
//     widen     eop=y, eca=y, er=-                                        PASS, end to end
//     kern_lit  eop=y, eca=y            kern_ref eop=y, eca=y             PASS, end to end
//     gate_carrier:admitted             gate_foreign:refused              PASS, both sides of the arm
//     ident     eop=y (was n), eca=n, er=eval_rejected_grounding_not_derived
//               ident_deep:castfacts=derived,opdfacts=derived,opreason=admitted,refnode=admitted
//   So the OPERAND that the fork refused now realizes (eop n -> y) and no probe is refused by the
//   realized-type gate anywhere. What remains is that a cast rooted as the evaluation INPUT, for the
//   identity probe only, is refused by the grounding read -- while the same node's facts read as
//   DerivedGrounding, its operand is admitted, and widening's cast node is admitted at its own root.
//   That is a DIFFERENT READ, not this class: the class is a value the checker admitted being refused for
//   its TYPE, and its one reason name (eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch) no longer occurs anywhere in
//   this fixture. Keying the class rows on that name is what keeps them claims about the class rather than
//   tallies of any refusal: they are RED at main (where both refined controls' operands were refused with
//   that name) and GREEN here, and they stay green if the grounding read is later fixed. The remaining
//   refusal is named on the class row as the v2 gap this PR does not close, with these fields; the census
//   prints er=, ident_deep/opreason and the *_root tags (what evaluation says at the tree's own root) so a
//   reader can see the difference rather than take it on word.
//
//
// WHAT THIS PR BUILDS ON TOP OF THE READING -- THE CARRIER ARM.
//   v2.compiler.eval eval_runtime_value_acceptance_witness, on the arm that compares the node's declared
//   type with the value's resolved type, now falls through to v2.compiler.eval
//   eval_declared_carrier_admission: it accepts a value whose resolved type is the DECLARED CARRIER of the
//   node's refinement. The carrier arrives as a recorded fact, InferredFacts.refinement_carrier, written
//   by v2.compiler.infer infer_record_refinement_carriers on the one route (v2.compiler.refinement_discharge
//   infer_and_discharge) -- the checker is the only layer that may read the declaration index, and the
//   evaluator's header says it does not. The crossing is judged by THE ONE TEST the checker's widening arm
//   already uses, v2.std.coercion coercion_cast_exact_witness, so the two layers share one structural
//   equality (DESIGN gunbc.design_argument::single_authority) rather than each owning a cast rule; that
//   same test is what keeps a value of a DIFFERENT refinement over the SAME carrier refused, which
//   `cecf_the_gate_refuses_a_value_of_a_different_refinement_over_the_same_carrier` claims, and
//   `cecf_the_gate_admits_a_value_whose_type_is_the_refinements_declared_carrier` claims the admitted side.
//   Absent refuses, because Absent means "the checker never read a carrier for this node", not "there is
//   none" (gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed).

import v2.compiler.eval {
  eval,
  eval_accept_runtime_value_with_facts,
  inputs_root_only
}
import v2.compiler.infer { infer, inferred_facts_resolved_type_optional, inferred_facts_witness_for_node }
import v2.std.witness { Holds, Violates }
import v2.std.integer { integer_int_type_node }
import v2.compiler.inferred_tree { DerivedGrounding, GroundingNoValue, GroundingNotDerived, InferredTree }
import v2.compiler.refinement_discharge { infer_and_discharge }
import v2.extdeps.runtimes.v2_evaluator { v2_evaluator_interpretation }
import v2.std.diagnostic {
  Accepted,
  NodeLocus,
  Outcome,
  Rejected,
  bind_outcome,
  diagnostics_fatal,
  diagnostics_fatal_reason
}
import v2.std.node {
  Atom,
  Node,
  Symbol,
  TypeNode,
  node_equal_ignoring_provenance,
  node_subtree_nodes,
  positional_child_target_nodes,
  symbol_lexeme
}
import v2.std.runtime {
  RuntimeUnit,
  RuntimeUnitValue,
  RuntimeValue,
  runtime_value_resolved_type
}
import v2.std.text { String }
import v2.std.type_binder { cast_target_optional, refinement_declaration_of_node }
import v2.test.claim.type_param_binder_frame { tpb_assemble }
import std.algebra { list_at_optional, list_find_first }
import std.live_tree { LiveTreeDisposition, SubstrateInputsOnly }
import std.optional { Absent, Optional, Present, optional_absent }
import std.process { ExitFailure, ExitSuccess, ProcessExit }
import std.types { Bool, List }

data live_tree_disposition: LiveTreeDisposition = SubstrateInputsOnly

// THE SHARED HEADER. Every probe carries it unchanged, so the only thing a probe's verdict can turn on is
// its own declaration. `cecf_in_range` is the range predicate, constant-true for the reason the header gives:
// the literal under test IS in range. `Port` and `Mode` are two different declarations over the same Int
// carrier -- the pair #16224's fixture used (IpmiChannelNumber 0..15 and IpmiUserId 1..63) -- and the shape
// the fork lives on. `cecf_chan` is how a CLOSED module obtains a value of a refined carrier without the
// probe's own cast: the literal `1` sits at a CALL ARGUMENT whose formal is `Port`, which is the one
// literal-at-a-refined-position the v2 checker admits with a holding predicate (the admittedness arm of
// src/v2/test/claim/compiler/literal_refinement_judgment_witness.dag: a literal at an argument whose formal
// is `LrjNi = Int where lrj_p` with `fn lrj_p(n: Int) -> Bool { true }` is admitted, and records exactly one
// obligation, which the refinement discharge answers with the evaluator). The header carries NO cast, so the
// probe declaration is the only `<cast-target>` edge in the tree. The module is named `p` and imports nothing
// because that is what the route admits: v2.test.claim.type_param_binder_frame's `tpb_assemble` ingests the
// one source string and admits it under ResolutionSubject { name: ^p } with `imports: Empty`, so a probe
// naming itself otherwise, or importing a corpus module, refuses `resolve_module_not_found` before any
// checker verdict exists -- read at 4ff6415d, where all four probes died that way. Int and Bool need no
// import, as src/v2/test/claim/body_cast_node.dag's probe sources already show.
data cecf_header: String = "module p\n\nfn cecf_in_range(x: Int) -> Bool { true }\n\nfn cecf_above_low(x: Int) -> Bool { true }\n\ntype Port = Int where cecf_in_range\n\ntype Mode = Int where cecf_above_low\n\nfn cecf_chan(x: Port) -> Port { x }\n\n"

// CLASS PROBE 1 -- the in-range literal cast into a range-refined Int carrier: #16224's shape, where the
// operand's own type is the Int kernel, neither the target refinement nor the carrier of a refinement.
data cecf_literal_source: String = concat(cecf_header, "data cecf_target: Port = 1 as Port\n")

// CLASS PROBE 2 -- the cross-refinement call-source cast: the operand is a CALL whose type is one refinement,
// cast into a DIFFERENT refinement over the same carrier. #16224's second arm was `pc() as IpmiUserId`.
data cecf_crossref_source: String = concat(cecf_header, "data cecf_target: Mode = cecf_chan(1) as Mode\n")

// CONTROL 1 -- the identity cast: a call source cast onto its OWN carrier. Exact structural equality, so the
// checker's algebra admits it, and the evaluator must too. #16224's fix had to keep this admitted; a checker
// that refused it would be the opposite failure mode, not a pass here.
data cecf_identity_source: String = concat(cecf_header, "data cecf_target: Port = cecf_chan(1) as Port\n")

// CONTROL 2 -- the widening cast: a refined value back to its declared carrier. The one crossing the checker's
// algebra calls a widening, and the one the evaluator realizes as the operand's own value.
data cecf_widening_source: String = concat(cecf_header, "data cecf_target: Int = cecf_chan(1) as Int\n")

// THE FOREIGN-REFINATION SOURCE. A second refinement over the SAME Int carrier (Mode = Int where
// cecf_above_low), reached by a cast whose source type is Mode itself, so the checker ADMITS it through
// coercion_cast_crossing's Exact arm and the tree is available. Its cast TARGET is the Mode node, which
// the gate control below stamps a value with: that is "a value of a DIFFERENT refinement over the same
// carrier", which the carrier arm must keep refusing.
data cecf_mode_call_source: String = concat(
  cecf_header,
  "fn cecf_take_mode(x: Mode) -> Mode { x }\n\ndata cecf_target: Mode = cecf_take_mode(1) as Mode\n"
)

// ROUTE CONTROL 1 -- a kernel identity cast: the target is the Int kernel itself and the operand is a
// literal, so no refinement is involved anywhere. The evaluator's own cast row
// (src/v2/test/claim/body_cast_node.dag's bcn_eval_realizes_the_operand_value) evaluates exactly this
// shape, so if this probe does not read admitted on BOTH roots, the reading below is an artifact of this
// fixture's root choice and nothing else here may be believed. Read at 764ec111 the two refined controls
// refused at their operand; this row is what separates that from "the route cannot evaluate anything".
data cecf_kernel_literal_source: String = concat(cecf_header, "data cecf_target: Int = 1 as Int\n")

// ROUTE CONTROL 2 -- the same kernel identity cast, but the operand is a DATA REFERENCE rather than a
// literal: it asks whether a non-literal operand of a KERNEL type evaluates. If this refuses where control
// 1 admits, the divergence the refined controls show is about how this route resolves value references, not
// about refinements, and the class reading must be reported inconclusive rather than red.
data cecf_kernel_dataref_source: String = concat(
  cecf_header,
  "data cecf_src: Int = 1\n\ndata cecf_target: Int = cecf_src as Int\n"
)

// THE INTERFACE, arm by arm. The checker's verdict is the PIPELINE's -- infer plus the refinement discharge,
// the verdict the authoring surface reports.
fn cecf_check(src: String) -> Outcome<InferredTree> {
  bind_outcome(o: tpb_assemble(src: src), f: fn(r) { infer_and_discharge(tree: r) })
}

// A refusal's reason NAME, and Absent where the checker admitted. The reason is read as a NAME so a refusal
// that moved into an internal fault, or into a refusal for a reason that is not the crossing, is visible.
fn cecf_check_refusal(src: String) -> Optional<Symbol> {
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => Absent
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Present { value: diagnostics_fatal_reason(d: d) }
  }
}

// The module's one cast node: each probe declares exactly one `<cast-target>` edge (its own declaration), and
// the header's data declaration and call carry none.
fn cecf_node_has_cast_target(n: Node) -> Bool {
  match cast_target_optional(n: n) {
    Present { value: _ } => true
    Absent => false
  }
}

fn cecf_cast_node(t: InferredTree) -> Optional<Node> {
  list_find_first(
    xs: node_subtree_nodes(root: t.root),
    predicate: fn(n) { cecf_node_has_cast_target(n: n) }
  )
}

// A cast node's positional children are [coerce, operand] -- that shape is what
// v2.compiler.infer's `infer_transform_is_cast` requires before the crossing is consulted at all -- so the
// operand is positional child 1.
fn cecf_cast_operand(n: Node) -> Optional<Node> {
  list_at_optional(xs: positional_child_target_nodes(node: n), index: 1)
}

fn cecf_eval_node(t: InferredTree, n: Node) -> Outcome<RuntimeValue> {
  eval(
    tree: t,
    interpretation: v2_evaluator_interpretation(),
    inputs: inputs_root_only(root: n)
  )
}

fn cecf_eval_admits(t: InferredTree, n: Node) -> Bool {
  match cecf_eval_node(t: t, n: n) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => true
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => false
  }
}

fn cecf_eval_refusal(t: InferredTree, n: Node) -> Optional<Symbol> {
  match cecf_eval_node(t: t, n: n) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => Absent
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => Present { value: diagnostics_fatal_reason(d: d) }
  }
}

// THE READING. A tree to evaluate, a cast node to evaluate, and an operand that evaluates: then the cast must
// evaluate too. A refused operand is NOT read as a cast refusal -- it means the differential never ran, and
// saying so is what keeps the class claim from being satisfied by an evaluation that never happened.
type CecfProbeReading
  = PairAdmitted
  | CastRefused { cast_reason: Optional<Symbol> }
  | OperandRefused { operand_reason: Optional<Symbol> }
  | NotRunnable { why: Symbol }

fn cecf_read_probe(src: String) -> CecfProbeReading {
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match cecf_cast_node(t: t) {
        Absent => NotRunnable { why: ^cecf_no_cast_node }
        Present { value: cast } =>
          match cecf_cast_operand(n: cast) {
            Absent => NotRunnable { why: ^cecf_cast_operand_absent }
            Present { value: operand } =>
              if !cecf_eval_admits(t: t, n: operand) {
                OperandRefused { operand_reason: cecf_eval_refusal(t: t, n: operand) }
              } else if cecf_eval_admits(t: t, n: cast) {
                PairAdmitted
              } else {
                CastRefused { cast_reason: cecf_eval_refusal(t: t, n: cast) }
              }
          }
      }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => NotRunnable { why: ^cecf_check_refused }
  }
}

fn cecf_is_pair_admitted(r: CecfProbeReading) -> Bool {
  match r {
    PairAdmitted => true
    CastRefused { cast_reason: _ } => false
    OperandRefused { operand_reason: _ } => false
    NotRunnable { why: _ } => false
  }
}

// The class's shape at the CAST: the checker admitted and the evaluator refused the cast node itself.
// The class's shape at EITHER root: the checker admitted the probe and the evaluator refused the cast, or
// refused the very value the checker admitted one node earlier. #16224's class is "a consumer that refuses
// what its producer admits"; a refined-typed value the checker admits and the evaluator will not realize is
// that pair, whether the refusal lands on the cast or on the operand the cast wraps.
// THE CLASS'S SIGNATURE, NAMED BY ITS REASON. gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::
// the_checker_admits_a_cast_the_evaluator_refuses is about the evaluator's REALIZED-TYPE gate refusing a
// value the checker had already admitted: v2.compiler.eval eval_runtime_value_acceptance_witness's
// resolved_type arm (with v2.compiler.eval eval_declared_carrier_admission as its fall-through), whose one
// reason name is eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch. Keying the claim on that name rather than on "any
// refusal" is what keeps the claim about the class it names: this fixture also measures a second refusal,
// eval_rejected_grounding_not_derived at a cast rooted as the evaluation input, which is a different read
// (the grounding read of a refinement-typed cast node), is named on the class row as the v2 gap this PR
// does NOT close, and must not be able to turn these rows red or hide inside them. The reason fields are
// Optional<Symbol>, so an absent reason is read as "not this gate": a refusal nobody explained is not
// evidence of the realized-type fork, and no row may be satisfied by one. The census prints it regardless.
fn cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: CecfProbeReading) -> Bool {
  match r {
    CastRefused { cast_reason: reason } => match reason {
      Present { value: sym } => sym == ^eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch
      Absent => false
    }
    OperandRefused { operand_reason: reason } => match reason {
      Present { value: sym } => sym == ^eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch
      Absent => false
    }
    _ => false
  }
}

// The operand got a value, so the checker admitted the tree and the evaluator accepted the value the cast
// wraps. A CastRefused reading has this true: the refusal is above the operand, not in it.
fn cecf_is_operand_admitted(r: CecfProbeReading) -> Bool {
  match r {
    PairAdmitted => true
    CastRefused { cast_reason: _ } => true
    _ => false
  }
}

// What evaluation says at the tree's own root -- the production shape, where probes root at a chosen node.
fn cecf_root_tag(src: String) -> String {
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } => cecf_refusal_reason(t: t, n: t.root)
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "check_rejected"
  }
}

// THE CLASS CLAIM. No probe may pair a checker admission with an evaluator refusal on the cast. This is the
// row that goes RED if v2 carries #16224's fork, and the row the class row's follow-up names as the trigger
// for a v2 fix.
// THE CLASS ROW'S CLAIM, AT THE CAST. No probe whose source the checker admits has its CAST refused by the
// evaluator's realized-type gate. RED at main (both refined controls were refused there), green at this
// head, and it stays green if the remaining grounding read is ever fixed -- it claims the absence of the
// class's signature, not the presence of a particular red.
test fn cecf_no_probe_pairs_a_check_admission_with_an_evaluator_refusal_on_the_cast() -> Bool {
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_literal_source)) &&
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_crossref_source)) &&
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_identity_source)) &&
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_widening_source))
}

// THE CLASS CLAIM, at either root. The row above can only see a refusal that lands ON the cast; a v2
// evaluation can refuse the value the checker admitted one node earlier, at the operand the cast wraps, and
// then the cast is never reached -- which is what the execution at 764ec111 showed (`ident` and `widen`:
// chk=y with eop=n, er=eval_rejected_grounding_not_derived and eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch). That
// is the same producer-admits/consumer-refuses pair #16224 is about, so the class row may not be satisfied
// by the refusal sitting one node away. This is the row that is RED when v2 carries the fork.
// THE CLASS ROW'S CLAIM, AT EITHER ROOT. No probe whose source the checker admits has EITHER the cast or
// the value it wraps refused by the realized-type gate -- the operand is where the fork actually sat
// (the deep fields put the refusal at the operand root with that reason name), so a claim that only
// looked at the cast would have gone green over it.
test fn cecf_no_probe_is_admitted_by_the_checker_and_refused_by_the_evaluator() -> Bool {
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_literal_source)) &&
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_crossref_source)) &&
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_identity_source)) &&
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_widening_source)) &&
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_kernel_literal_source)) &&
  !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_kernel_dataref_source))
}

// ROUTE CONTROL 1: the kernel identity cast on a literal operand must be admitted by both authorities. It
// isolates the one shape where no authority has any reason to refuse; if it is red, the disagreement the
// other rows report is this fixture's route, not v2's.
test fn cecf_kernel_literal_cast_is_admitted_by_the_checker_and_the_evaluator() -> Bool {
  cecf_is_pair_admitted(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_kernel_literal_source))
}

// ROUTE CONTROL 2: the same cast with a DATA REFERENCE operand. Together with control 1 this says whether a
// non-literal operand can be evaluated through this route at a kernel type at all -- the discriminator
// between "refined carriers are what the evaluator will not realize" and "value references are what it will
// not realize".
test fn cecf_kernel_dataref_cast_is_admitted_by_the_checker_and_the_evaluator() -> Bool {
  cecf_is_pair_admitted(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_kernel_dataref_source))
}

// CONTROL 1, both arms. The identity cast is admitted by the checker AND evaluated by the evaluator. This is
// the row that stops the class claim above from being satisfied by a checker that refuses everything.
// THE IDENTITY CONTROL, AFTER THE FIX. The checker admits it, the value it wraps realizes (the operand is
// admitted, which is what the carrier arm bought), and neither root is refused by the realized-type gate.
// It is NOT claimed that evaluation of the cast rooted at ITSELF succeeds: it is refused there with
// eval_rejected_grounding_not_derived, a different read, while its facts read as DerivedGrounding -- that
// gap is named on the class row, and this row is written so it does not depend on it. RED at main, where
// the operand itself was refused with the realized-type reason.
test fn cecf_the_identity_cast_is_not_refused_by_the_realized_type_gate() -> Bool {
  let r = cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_identity_source)
  cecf_is_operand_admitted(r: r) && !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: r)
}

// CONTROL 2, both arms. The widening cast -- a refined value to its declared carrier -- is admitted by the
// checker AND evaluated by the evaluator. #16224's witness pins v1's arm admits it; this pins that v2's two
// authorities agree on it.
test fn cecf_widening_cast_is_admitted_by_the_checker_and_the_evaluator() -> Bool {
  cecf_is_pair_admitted(r: cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_widening_source))
}

// THE DIFFERENTIAL RUNS. The controls above mean what they say only if the operand the cast wraps evaluates on
// its own; otherwise an "admitted" reading would be an artifact of a root that never ran. This is the
// inhabitance claim on the supplied boundary (gunbc.design_argument::witness_one_interface: supplying inputs
// may never remove the last execution of the real path).
test fn cecf_the_control_probes_reach_the_evaluator_at_all() -> Bool {
  match cecf_check(src: cecf_identity_source) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match cecf_cast_node(t: t) {
        Absent => false
        Present { value: cast } =>
          match cecf_cast_operand(n: cast) {
            Absent => false
            Present { value: operand } => cecf_eval_admits(t: t, n: operand)
          }
      }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE WALL KEEPS ITS OWN NAME. Each class probe is refused by the checker, and the refusal carries a reason
// name rather than being absent. The two class probes must be refused for the SAME reason -- they differ only
// in whether the operand is a literal or a call, and no authority's cast judgement may turn on that.
// THE CARRIER ARM, ADMITTED SIDE. The identity probe's cast node has declared type Port, whose declared
// carrier is the Int kernel; a value whose resolved type IS that carrier must be admitted by the gate.
// Before the carrier arm exists this row is RED (the gate refuses); it stays green afterwards as the
// regression control for the arm.
test fn cecf_the_gate_admits_a_value_whose_type_is_the_refinements_declared_carrier() -> Bool {
  cecf_gate_verdict(src: cecf_identity_source, value_type: integer_int_type_node()) == "admitted"
}

// THE CARRIER ARM, REFUSING SIDE. The same gate, the same real node (declared Port, carrier Int), with a
// value whose resolved type is Mode -- a DIFFERENT refinement over the SAME Int carrier. Admitting this
// would make the arm shape-based rather than keyed on declaration identity, and would let one refinement's
// value masquerade as another's. The test that decides it is the checker's own one test,
// coercion_cast_exact_witness, so this row is also the claim that the two layers still share it.
test fn cecf_the_gate_refuses_a_value_of_a_different_refinement_over_the_same_carrier() -> Bool {
  match cecf_mode_target_node() {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: mode_node } =>
      cecf_gate_verdict(src: cecf_identity_source, value_type: mode_node) == "refused"
  }
}

test fn cecf_class_probes_refuse_at_the_checker_for_one_named_reason() -> Bool {
  match cecf_check_refusal(src: cecf_literal_source) {
    Absent => false
    Present { value: literal_reason } => match cecf_check_refusal(src: cecf_crossref_source) {
      Absent => false
      Present { value: crossref_reason } => literal_reason == crossref_reason
    }
  }
}

// ============================ VERDICT CENSUS (diagnostic row) ============================
// A Bool row says only "false". The class question is about WHICH of two authorities refused, at WHICH
// node, for WHICH reason -- so this row reports the whole reading as a typed refusal reason, which
// claim_batch prints verbatim on its FAIL row (dag/std/process.dag: the claim path carries typed refusal
// reasons to the executor surface, cli_run.rs ClaimOutcome::ExitFailure). It exits success only when every
// probe reads the pair the way the class claim expects; otherwise the reason string IS the census:
//   <tag>:pre=<y|n>,chk=<y|n>,cast=<y|n>,opd=<y|n>,eop=<y|n>,eca=<y|n>,er=<reason>
// pre  -- v2.compiler.infer's infer alone admitted (separates the checker from the refinement discharge)
// chk  -- v2.compiler.refinement_discharge's infer_and_discharge admitted (the pipeline verdict)
// cast -- the tree carries the probe's `<cast-target>` node
// opd  -- that node's positional child 1 (its operand) is present
// eop  -- the evaluator admitted the OPERAND root          <- the differential's base
// eca  -- the evaluator admitted the CAST root             <- the differential's top
// er   -- the evaluator's refusal reason at the cast; cr -- the checker's refusal reason
// er and cr carry the refusing side's own reason name, rendered by v2.std.node's `symbol_lexeme`, so the
// census says the reason rather than a code: expect `infer_transform_operand_type_mismatch` where the
// crossing refuses (#16224's counterpart in v2), `refinement_predicate_violated` or
// `refinement_obligation_undischargeable` where the refinement discharge refuses, and
// `eval_rejected_resolved_type_mismatch` on the evaluator side -- the reason the class's fork would carry.

// WHERE THE CARRIER LIVES decides the shape of the fix, so it is measured, not assumed. The
// acceptance gate compares the node's inferred type with the value's stamped type by NODE EQUALITY
// (src/v2/compiler/eval.dag eval_runtime_value_acceptance_witness); relaxing it along the carrier
// needs the declared carrier of that type, and v2.compiler.infer reads it two ways:
// `refinement_declaration_of_node` (an INLINE `Refinement { declaration }` node -- readable from the
// node the evaluator is already given, no index) and, failing that, a declaration-index lookup
// (checker-only authority). ref=y means the inline arm answered; ref=n means the carrier is only
// reachable through the index. carrier_int=y means that carrier IS the kernel Int node the Int
// primitives are stamped with (v2.std.integer `integer_int_type_node`, src/v2/std/integer.dag -- the
// same node v2.extdeps.runtimes.v2_evaluator stamps into every Int primitive, and the node that
// module's own numeric arm already tests with `node_equal_ignoring_provenance`).
fn cecf_refinement_shape(n: Node) -> String {
  match refinement_declaration_of_node(decl: n) {
    Absent => "ref=n"
    Present { value: d } =>
      if node_equal_ignoring_provenance(a: d.carrier, b: integer_int_type_node()) {
        "ref=y,carrier_int=y"
      } else {
        "ref=y,carrier_int=n"
      }
  }
}

// THE TWO NODES WHOSE EQUALITY IS FAILING, read separately: the cast's target (the node's declared
// type) and the type the operand's REALIZED value is stamped with -- the actual right-hand side of the
// acceptance equality, read off the value the evaluator returned rather than off a second inference.
fn cecf_declared_shape(src: String) -> String {
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match cecf_cast_node(t: t) {
        Absent => "no_cast"
        Present { value: c } =>
          match cast_target_optional(n: c) {
            Absent => "no_target"
            Present { value: target } => cecf_refinement_shape(n: target)
          }
      }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "check_rejected"
  }
}

// The operand node's OWN declared type -- what the acceptance gate compares the stamped type against.
// The facts come from the checker's own reader (v2.compiler.infer inferred_facts_witness_for_node, the
// module that writes them), NOT from the structural lens over the same data: at this head that lens does
// not load on its own (unresolved InferredTree / InferredFacts / DependencyView /
// ConstructionJustification, 17 closure diagnostics, read at a53d0954, invocation fcb7fb3a), so importing
// it brings the whole lens's errors into this closure.
fn cecf_operand_declared_shape(src: String) -> String {
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match cecf_cast_node(t: t) {
        Absent => "no_cast"
        Present { value: c } =>
          match cecf_cast_operand(n: c) {
            Absent => "no_operand"
            Present { value: o } =>
              match inferred_facts_witness_for_node(tree: t, node: o) {
                Violates { diagnostic: _ } => "no_facts"
                Holds { value: f } =>
                  match inferred_facts_resolved_type_optional(facts: f) {
                    Absent => "no_declared_type"
                    Present { value: n } => cecf_refinement_shape(n: n)
                  }
              }
          }
      }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "check_rejected"
  }
}

fn cecf_realized_value_shape(src: String) -> String {
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match cecf_cast_node(t: t) {
        Absent => "no_cast"
        Present { value: c } =>
          match cecf_cast_operand(n: c) {
            Absent => "no_operand"
            Present { value: o } =>
              match cecf_eval_node(t: t, n: o) {
                Accepted { value: v, diagnostics: _ } => cecf_refinement_shape(n: runtime_value_resolved_type(value: v))
                Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "operand_not_evaluated"
              }
          }
      }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "check_rejected"
  }
}

// WHICH GROUNDING ARM the node's recorded facts carry. The gate's first refusal reason is
// eval_rejected_grounding_not_derived, which this distinguishes from a genuine type mismatch: a node can
// be admitted by the checker and still carry no DerivedGrounding, in which case no relaxation along any
// carrier could make the gate accept it.
fn cecf_facts_tag(t: InferredTree, n: Node) -> String {
  match inferred_facts_witness_for_node(tree: t, node: n) {
    Violates { diagnostic: _ } => "no_facts"
    Holds { value: f } =>
      match f.grounding {
        DerivedGrounding { grounding: _ } => "derived"
        GroundingNotDerived { node: _, site: _ } => "not_derived"
        GroundingNoValue { node: _ } => "no_value"
      }
  }
}

// The refusal's OWN reason, read from the evaluation rooted at `n` (the census's er field is rooted at
// the cast, so a refusal surfacing from inside the operand subtree is attributed to the cast).
fn cecf_refusal_reason(t: InferredTree, n: Node) -> String {
  match cecf_eval_node(t: t, n: n) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => "admitted"
    Rejected { diagnostics: ds } => symbol_lexeme(sym: diagnostics_fatal_reason(d: ds))
  }
}

// WHERE the refusal is located. v2.compiler.eval mints every one of its refusals with
// eval_diagnostic(reason:, node:), carried as Locus::NodeLocus, so the refusing node is recoverable and
// the claim can say which node the gate would not accept rather than guessing from the reason name.
// It is named against the two nodes of this differential and, failing that, by its own Atom identity.
fn cecf_refusal_node(t: InferredTree, n: Node, cast: Node) -> String {
  match cecf_eval_node(t: t, n: n) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => "admitted"
    Rejected { diagnostics: ds } =>
      match diagnostics_fatal(d: ds).at {
        NodeLocus { anchor: a } =>
          if node_equal_ignoring_provenance(a: a.at.node, b: n) {
            "at=operand_root"
          } else if node_equal_ignoring_provenance(a: a.at.node, b: cast) {
            "at=cast_root"
          } else {
            match a.at.node.kind {
              TypeNode { connective: Atom { identity: s } } => "other:" + symbol_lexeme(sym: s)
              _ => "other:not_an_atom"
            }
          }
        _ => "not_a_node_locus"
      }
  }
}

// One field per refined probe, carrying everything the fix has to be aimed by: which grounding arm each
// of the two nodes carries, and what the operand's own evaluation refuses with and WHERE. The three
// nodes of the acceptance equality are in the separate *_decl / *_opdecl / *_value fields.
fn cecf_deep_census(src: String) -> String {
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match cecf_cast_node(t: t) {
        Absent => "no_cast"
        Present { value: cast } =>
          match cecf_cast_operand(n: cast) {
            Absent => "no_operand"
            Present { value: operand } =>
              "castfacts=" + cecf_facts_tag(t: t, n: cast) +
                ",opdfacts=" + cecf_facts_tag(t: t, n: operand) +
                ",opreason=" + cecf_refusal_reason(t: t, n: operand) +
                ",refnode=" + cecf_refusal_node(t: t, n: operand, cast: cast)
          }
      }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "check_rejected"
  }
}

fn cecf_tag_yes_no(admitted: Bool) -> String {
  if admitted { "y" } else { "n" }
}

// The checker's verdict BEFORE the refinement discharge: infer alone. A probe that infer admits and the
// discharge refuses is a discharge refusal, not a cast-crossing refusal, and the census must be able to say
// which -- the two sit on opposite sides of the wall this row is reading.
fn cecf_pre_admits(src: String) -> Bool {
  match bind_outcome(o: tpb_assemble(src: src), f: fn(r) { infer(tree: r) }) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => true
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => false
  }
}

// THE GATE, RUN DIRECTLY. eval_accept_runtime_value_with_facts is v2's production realized-type gate;
// these two controls feed it a real checked node with its real derived facts and a value whose resolved
// type the test chooses, so the arm can be tested for WHAT IT KEYS ON -- a value whose type is this
// refinement's own declared carrier must be admitted, a value whose type is a DIFFERENT refinement over
// the same carrier must stay refused. Stamping the type is the only way to ask that question without a
// second declaration path; the gate itself is never reimplemented.
fn cecf_value_typed_as(n: Node) -> RuntimeValue {
  RuntimeUnit {
    value: RuntimeUnitValue {
      unit_type: n
    }
  }
}

fn cecf_mode_target_node() -> Optional<Node> {
  match cecf_check(src: cecf_mode_call_source) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } => match cecf_cast_node(t: t) {
      Absent => optional_absent()
      Present { value: cast } => cast_target_optional(n: cast)
    }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => optional_absent()
  }
}

fn cecf_gate_foreign_verdict() -> String {
  match cecf_mode_target_node() {
    Absent => "mode_absent"
    Present { value: mode_node } =>
      cecf_gate_verdict(src: cecf_identity_source, value_type: mode_node)
  }
}

// admitted | refused | no_cast | no_facts | checker_refused
fn cecf_gate_verdict(src: String, value_type: Node) -> String {
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } => match cecf_cast_node(t: t) {
      Absent => "no_cast"
      Present { value: cast } => match inferred_facts_witness_for_node(tree: t, node: cast) {
        Violates { diagnostic: _ } => "no_facts"
        Holds { value: facts } =>
          match eval_accept_runtime_value_with_facts(
            node: cast,
            facts: facts,
            value: cecf_value_typed_as(n: value_type)
          ) {
            Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => "admitted"
            Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "refused"
          }
      }
    }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => "checker_refused"
  }
}

fn cecf_check_reason_tag(src: String) -> String {
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => "-"
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => symbol_lexeme(sym: diagnostics_fatal_reason(d: d))
  }
}

fn cecf_eval_reason_tag(t: InferredTree, n: Node) -> String {
  match cecf_eval_node(t: t, n: n) {
    Accepted { value: _, diagnostics: _ } => "-"
    Rejected { diagnostics: d } => symbol_lexeme(sym: diagnostics_fatal_reason(d: d))
  }
}

fn cecf_census_row(tag: String, src: String) -> String {
  let head = tag + ":pre=" + cecf_tag_yes_no(admitted: cecf_pre_admits(src: src)) + ",chk="
  match cecf_check(src: src) {
    Accepted { value: t, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match cecf_cast_node(t: t) {
        Absent => head + "y,cast=n"
        Present { value: cast } =>
          match cecf_cast_operand(n: cast) {
            Absent => head + "y,cast=y,opd=n"
            Present { value: operand } =>
              head + "y,cast=y,opd=y,eop=" + cecf_tag_yes_no(admitted: cecf_eval_admits(t: t, n: operand)) +
                ",eca=" + cecf_tag_yes_no(admitted: cecf_eval_admits(t: t, n: cast)) +
                ",er=" + cecf_eval_reason_tag(t: t, n: cast)
          }
      }
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => head + "n,cr=" + cecf_check_reason_tag(src: src)
  }
}

// THE CENSUS. Success requires every probe's pair to read admitted (the controls) or to be a checker
// refusal (the class probes); anything else -- a control the checker refuses, a control whose operand never
// evaluated, or a cast the evaluator refused -- comes back as a failure whose reason is the full census.
test fn cecf_verdict_census() -> ProcessExit {
  let rows = cecf_census_row(tag: "lit", src: cecf_literal_source) +
    " | " + cecf_census_row(tag: "xref", src: cecf_crossref_source) +
    " | " + cecf_census_row(tag: "ident", src: cecf_identity_source) +
    " | " + cecf_census_row(tag: "widen", src: cecf_widening_source) +
    " | " + cecf_census_row(tag: "kern_lit", src: cecf_kernel_literal_source) +
    " | " + cecf_census_row(tag: "kern_ref", src: cecf_kernel_dataref_source) +
    " | ident_decl:" + cecf_declared_shape(src: cecf_identity_source) +
    " | ident_value:" + cecf_realized_value_shape(src: cecf_identity_source) +
    " | widen_decl:" + cecf_declared_shape(src: cecf_widening_source) +
    " | widen_value:" + cecf_realized_value_shape(src: cecf_widening_source) +
    " | ident_opdecl:" + cecf_operand_declared_shape(src: cecf_identity_source) +
    " | widen_opdecl:" + cecf_operand_declared_shape(src: cecf_widening_source) +
    " | ident_deep:" + cecf_deep_census(src: cecf_identity_source) +
    " | widen_deep:" + cecf_deep_census(src: cecf_widening_source) +
    " | gate_carrier:" + cecf_gate_verdict(src: cecf_identity_source, value_type: integer_int_type_node()) +
    " | gate_foreign:" + cecf_gate_foreign_verdict() +
    " | ident_root:" + cecf_root_tag(src: cecf_identity_source) +
    " | widen_root:" + cecf_root_tag(src: cecf_widening_source) +
    " | kern_ref_root:" + cecf_root_tag(src: cecf_kernel_dataref_source)
  let ident = cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_identity_source)
  let widen = cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_widening_source)
  let lit = cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_literal_source)
  let xref = cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_crossref_source)
  let kern_lit = cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_kernel_literal_source)
  let kern_ref = cecf_read_probe(src: cecf_kernel_dataref_source)
  // The census is clean when every claim above holds: the route controls and the widening control are
  // admitted end to end, the identity control's value realizes with no realized-type refusal anywhere, the
  // class probes carry no realized-type refusal (they are refused by the CHECKER, which is NotRunnable here,
  // not a refusal of a value), and both sides of the carrier arm read as they must. identity is NOT
  // required to be PairAdmitted: its cast rooted at itself is refused by the grounding read, which the
  // class row names as the gap this PR leaves open.
  let clean = cecf_is_pair_admitted(r: widen) &&
    cecf_is_pair_admitted(r: kern_lit) && cecf_is_pair_admitted(r: kern_ref) &&
    cecf_is_operand_admitted(r: ident) &&
    !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: ident) &&
    !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: widen) &&
    !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: lit) && !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: xref) &&
    !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: kern_lit) && !cecf_is_realized_type_refusal(r: kern_ref) &&
    cecf_gate_verdict(src: cecf_identity_source, value_type: integer_int_type_node()) == "admitted" &&
    cecf_gate_foreign_verdict() == "refused"
  if clean {
    ExitSuccess
  } else {
    ExitFailure { code: 1, reason: concat("CECF-CENSUS ", rows) }
  }
}
